<Docs
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install cairn, create a project, and work through the loop it is built around."
  summary="Install it, create a project, and work through the loop it is built around: find something to do, take it, finish it."
>
  <h2>Install</h2>

  <p>A prebuilt binary, with no Rust toolchain needed:</p>

  <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oddurs/cairn/main/install.sh | sh</code></pre>

  <p>
    It detects your platform, verifies the release checksum and installs to
    <code>~/.local/bin</code>. Homebrew takes three commands, because recent
    versions require third-party taps to be trusted explicitly:
  </p>

  <pre><code>brew tap oddurs/cairn
brew trust oddurs/cairn
brew install cairn</code></pre>

  <p>
    From source, <code>cargo install --git https://github.com/oddurs/cairn
    cairn-md</code>. The package is called <code>cairn-md</code> because
    <code>cairn</code> belongs to an unrelated crate; the binary it installs is
    <code>cairn</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Create a project</h2>

  <pre><code>$ cairn init
created cairn.toml
created cairn/items/</code></pre>

  <p>
    That writes the schema and the directory items live in. Run it at the root
    of a repository — items are meant to be committed alongside the code they
    describe.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>cairn init --preset minimal</code> starts from three statuses and
    nothing else. The default writes a fully commented schema to delete from,
    which is usually the faster way to arrive somewhere that fits.
  </p>

  <h2>The loop</h2>

  <p>
    Four commands carry the day-to-day. Everything else is machinery underneath
    them.
  </p>

  <pre><code>$ cairn next
ID    PRIORITY  STATUS       MILESTONE  TITLE
0001  p0        in progress  v0.1       Support OAuth login
0003  p1        backlog      v0.1       Board shears on narrow terminals

2 ready · 1 in progress · 1 blocked</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>cairn next</code> answers what to do now. It excludes anything waiting
    on unfinished work, and puts what is already under way first — the most
    useful next action is usually finishing something.
  </p>

  <pre><code>$ cairn claim --next
claimed 0001  Support OAuth login
  you · doing · cairn/items/0001-support-oauth-login.md</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>cairn claim</code> writes your name into the item and moves it to an
    active status, so nobody starts the same work twice. It refuses an item
    somebody else holds, and one that is blocked.
  </p>

  <pre><code>$ cairn note 1 "Chose the authorization code flow; implicit is deprecated."
$ cairn close 1</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>cairn note</code> appends to the item under a dated heading. A status
    records what was decided; a note records why, which is the difference
    between a backlog and a list of things nobody remembers rejecting.
  </p>

  <h2>What gets written down</h2>

  <p>An item is a Markdown file, and that is the whole storage format:</p>

  <pre><code>---
id: 1
title: Support OAuth login
type: feature
status: doing
milestone: v0.1
priority: p0
---

## Problem

Password auth is the only option.</code></pre>

  <p>
    Everything above the closing <code>---</code> is checked against the schema.
    Everything below it is yours and is never interpreted. Edit the file in your
    editor or from the command line — both keep working, and changing a title
    renames the file to match.
  </p>

  <h2>The generated roadmap</h2>

  <p>
    <code>ROADMAP.md</code> is built from the items, and rebuilt whenever one
    changes. Never edit it by hand.
  </p>

  <pre><code>$ cairn render
wrote ROADMAP.md  14 items

$ cairn check --render --strict
ok: 14 item(s), 0 warning(s)</code></pre>

  <p>
    That second command belongs in continuous integration. It validates every
    item against the schema and proves the committed roadmap still matches them,
    which is what stops a roadmap from quietly becoming fiction.
  </p>

